+### 1. SPEC_GAP + CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION — verdict is derived by a bare substring scan, APPROVED first (reproduced)
+
+`scripts/compound-v-dogfood-index.sh:98-108`. The anchored pattern at `:52` correctly *finds* the line, but the
+value is then taken by scanning the **whole line** for `APPROVED` before `ISSUES`:
+
+```sh
+case "$line" in
+  *[Aa][Pp][Pp][Rr][Oo][Vv][Ee][Dd]*) verdict="APPROVED" ;;
+  *[Ii][Ss][Ss][Uu][Ee][Ss]*)         verdict="ISSUES" ;;
+```
+
+The spec's Parsing rule defines `verdict` as the value the anchored alternation `(APPROVED|ISSUES)` matched.
+Archaeology §6.8 says the anchor exists precisely so that a bare substring search cannot decide the verdict — this
+reintroduces that search one scope inward. Any `ISSUES` line that also contains the word "approved" is silently
+indexed `APPROVED`, which archaeology §6.7 calls out as the failure mode that becomes "a wrong, merged, published
+claim with nothing to catch it downstream."
+
+Reproduced live. Fixture `2026-01-01-probe-review.md`:
+
+```
+VERDICT: ISSUES — the earlier pass was approved, this one is not.
+```
+
+Generator output:
+
+```
+| date | feature | pass | verdict | file |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 2026-01-01 | probe | 1 | APPROVED | 2026-01-01-probe-review.md |
+
+Reviews: 1 · APPROVED: 1 · ISSUES: 0 · other: 0
+```
+
+→ Derive the verdict from the matched token, not the line — e.g. capture the anchored alternation
+(`grep -m1 -ioE -- "$verdict_pattern"` and classify the *last word* of that match), so text after the verdict value
+cannot override it. Add a fixture with an `ISSUES` line containing the word "approved". No corpus file triggers
+this today, so `README.md` needs no regeneration for it.
+
